{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789234490,"nonce":"cTVDZuRKhPiBSsCv9pxNHoJlSmI=","title":"A local named like a function resolves to the function","message":"def f(x: i64): i64 { return x }\n    def g(): i64 {\n        let f = 3\n        let h: i64 = f\n        return h\n    }\n\nfails to lower with \"'f' takes 1 argument but is called with 0 in\n'g'\": the read of the local `f` is resolved as a reference to the\nfunction `f`. A local must shadow a function of the same name inside\nits scope. Found when a Python program defined `f` and a prelude\nfunction used `f` as a local.","files":null}]}